 since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-330348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-330353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=8u(t)+7v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(8{e}^{-t})^{′} \\\\ (7{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-76598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-7{e}^{-t} \\\\ 8{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{t} \\\\ 7{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-t} \\\\ -7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 7{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=-2u(t)+7v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-2{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (7{e}^{5t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 35{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-76600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{3t} \\\\ 21{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{5t} \\\\ 21{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 7{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 35{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{5t} \\\\ 35{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 35{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-168100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If\n$u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-168100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-168100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-168100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field