_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-39726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=-2u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-2{e}^{-t})^{′} \\\\ (-{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-39726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-39726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-39726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-39726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{7t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=2u(t)+3v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{7t})^{′} \\\\ (3{e}^{-2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14{e}^{7t} \\\\ -6{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-76601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 21{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{7t} \\\\ 14{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}14{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -6{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}14{e}^{7t} \\\\ -6{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{7t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-76601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}14{e}^{7t} \\\\ -6{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $5$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-330300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972875cb7ed570621eb3dc3196d9e76b3ef34c948aee263a74c9c045f4ab7dc3"} {"question" "q-330305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a8639649c43ccf473fc0212c91af3b4fdc66d39cbce10b1e9640246157aea6c"} {"question" "q-330305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Aca