ure-workers/differential/14066703/example-21110.json" "file_sha256" "e1862497f5f8f3265314df44fa75c1b3fee9f73fcc42d47710de5270b3d80b06"} {"question" "e-21110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ the linear combination \n\n$$\nc_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/example-21110.json" "file_sha256" "e1862497f5f8f3265314df44fa75c1b3fee9f73fcc42d47710de5270b3d80b06"} {"question" "e-21112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/32e9cba3d7ee75019489dcc8a9c82f1b854bfa77af49e22fa9f51d4412cba95d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/dc64b0c557c838cf31e652ebdb8eee91d1fee8c947b343b16fceaf765c1f8517.png) are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/64b157c381c05102b51320592e61ffff5c4d611af4bd15679f1f2b38f3d01b10.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/example-21112.json" "file_sha256" "2464c18ba651749652297c39a75d44784a02237097f8189e1ff93c25a6e0281e"} {"question" "e-21112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\nc\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).$ So, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6{e}^{5t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16{e}^{4t} \\\\ 30{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/example-21112.json" "file_sha256" "2464c18ba651749652297c39a75d44784a02237097f8189e1ff93c25a6e0281e"} {"question" "e-21111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/example-21111.json" "file_sha256" "cd72b8f3ac9b4677a6032cd194cc8cc6094a6a712508d7b184c713f619167af3"} {"question" "e-21111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),$ then $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t),$ $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/example-21111.json" "file_sha256" "cd72b8f3ac9b4677a6032cd194cc8cc6094a6a712508d7b184c713f619167af3"} {"question" "q-168100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "5ba6498f134c960642f4617925e5226871ee80717384054e716543bf8f43f923" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "Since w=u−v and both u and v satisfy x'=Ax, linearity gives Aw=u'−v'=w'. Differentiating the two components of w yields (cos t+sin t, cos t−sin t). Original option b (index 1) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option e was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. Stored 